CertHub.register({"id":"security-plus","vendor":"CompTIA","name":"CompTIA Security+","short":"Security+","exam":"SY0-701","blurb":"Baseline security certification: controls, threats, architecture, operations and governance.","status":"verified","statusNote":"Weights verified Sept 24, 2026. SY0-801 preview expected around Oct 20, 2026; SY0-701 expected to retire around May 2027.","lastVerified":"2026-09-24","notices":[{"from":"2026-09-24","until":"2026-10-20","text":"CompTIA expects to preview the next Security+ exam (SY0-801) around Oct 20, 2026. This plan follows SY0-701, the current exam."},{"from":"2026-10-21","until":"2027-05-31","text":"SY0-701 is expected to retire around May 2027. Book your exam before then, or switch to the SY0-801 objectives."}],"examInfo":{"questions":"Up to 90 (multiple choice and PBQs)","minutes":90,"pass":"750 on a 100–900 scale"},"examSim":{"questions":90,"minutes":90},"sources":[{"label":"CompTIA Security+ SY0-701 exam objectives","url":"https://www.comptia.org/certifications/security"}],"start":"2026-09-28","examDate":"2027-01-19","notesLabel":"Reread in your notes","videoTip":"Watch the matching videos (Professor Messer's free SY0-701 course works well)","domains":[{"id":1,"name":"General security concepts","w":12},{"id":2,"name":"Threats, vulnerabilities & mitigations","w":22},{"id":3,"name":"Security architecture","w":18},{"id":4,"name":"Security operations","w":28},{"id":5,"name":"Program management & oversight","w":20}],"checkpoints":[{"after":4,"dom":1},{"after":8,"dom":2},{"after":11,"dom":3},{"after":14,"dom":4},{"after":15,"dom":5}],"phases":[[1,4,"Foundations and Domain 1"],[5,8,"Threats (Domain 2)"],[9,11,"Architecture (Domain 3)"],[12,14,"Security operations (Domain 4)"],[15,15,"Governance (Domain 5)"],[16,16,"Final review"]],"weeks":[{"title":"Controls, CIA and zero trust","dom":1,"obj":"1.1, 1.2","topics":["Control categories: technical, managerial, operational, physical","Control types: preventive, deterrent, detective, corrective, compensating, directive","CIA triad, AAA, non-repudiation","Zero trust: control plane vs data plane, policy engine, PEP","Physical security and deception tech (honeypots, honeynets, honeytokens)"],"notes":["Security Fundamentals","Securing Servers Using Honeypots and IP Blocking"],"lab":"Rewrite your honeypot lab as a one-paragraph answer: which control category and type is a honeypot, and why?","study":[["Name one example of each control type (preventive, deterrent, detective, corrective, compensating) from a place you have worked.","Examples: preventive = firewall rule or badge door; deterrent = warning banner or visible camera; detective = SIEM alert or log review; corrective = restoring from backup; compensating = isolating a server you cannot patch."],["Explain zero trust to a non-technical lounge investor in two sentences.","Nobody is trusted just because they are inside the network. Every request is checked for who you are, what device you are on and what you need, every time."],["What does non-repudiation prove, and which technology provides it?","That a specific party sent or signed something and cannot credibly deny it. Digital signatures (private key signing) provide it."]]},{"title":"Change management and crypto basics","dom":1,"obj":"1.3, 1.4","topics":["Change management: approval, CAB, impact analysis, backout plan, maintenance window","Symmetric vs asymmetric encryption, key exchange","Hashing, salting, key stretching","Encryption levels: full disk, partition, file, database, record","Obfuscation: steganography, tokenization, data masking"],"notes":["Public Key Infrastructure","Unit 3 Assessment Review (hashing)"],"lab":"Hash the same file with sha256sum twice, change one character, hash again. Write down what changed and why that proves integrity.","study":[["You missed this in Unit 3: what is hashing actually for, and why is it not encryption?","Hashing verifies integrity. It is one-way, so you cannot recover the input; encryption is two-way and protects confidentiality."],["Why does a salt defeat rainbow tables?","A unique random salt per password means precomputed hash tables no longer match; the attacker would have to rebuild tables per salt."],["List the parts of a change request a CAB expects to see.","Purpose, scope, impact analysis, risk, test results, schedule/maintenance window, owner, approvals and a backout plan."]]},{"title":"PKI and certificates","dom":1,"obj":"1.4","topics":["Public/private keys, key escrow","Certificates: CA, CSR, root of trust, self-signed, wildcard, SAN","Revocation: CRL vs OCSP, OCSP stapling","Digital signatures","TPM, HSM, secure enclave, key management system"],"notes":["Public Key Infrastructure","Understanding PKI","Block 08 Review"],"lab":"Open any HTTPS site in your browser, view its certificate chain and identify the root CA, intermediate, validity dates and SAN entries.","study":[["Walk through how a digital signature is created and verified.","Sender hashes the message and encrypts the hash with their private key. Receiver decrypts with the sender's public key, hashes the message themselves and compares."],["When would you choose a SAN certificate over a wildcard?","When you need to cover different domain names (example.com and example.net) or multiple levels, not just first-level subdomains of one domain."],["TPM vs HSM in one line each.","TPM: chip in one device storing keys for that device (boot integrity, disk encryption). HSM: dedicated appliance or card managing keys at scale for servers/CAs."]]},{"title":"Network foundations + Checkpoint 1","dom":1,"obj":"Ports and protocols used across all domains","topics":["OSI layers and where attacks happen","Secure vs insecure protocols: SSH/Telnet, SFTP/FTP, LDAPS/LDAP, HTTPS/HTTP, SNMPv3","Key ports: 22, 25, 53, 80, 443, 389, 636, 3389","ARP, DNS, DHCP and their attacks","Checkpoint test: Domain 1"],"notes":["Introduction to Computer Network","Layer 1: Physical","Layers 5-7","Block 05–07 Reviews"],"lab":"Make a two-column flashcard sheet: insecure protocol and port on the left, secure replacement and port on the right.","study":[["PBQ drill: match each service to its port — SSH, DNS, SMTP, LDAPS, RDP, HTTPS.","SSH 22, DNS 53, SMTP 25, LDAPS 636, RDP 3389, HTTPS 443."],["How does ARP poisoning enable an on-path attack?","The attacker sends forged ARP replies mapping their MAC to the gateway's IP, so victims send traffic through the attacker."],["Which protocols would you disable on a hardened server and what replaces them?","Telnet → SSH, FTP → SFTP/FTPS, HTTP → HTTPS, SNMPv1/2c → SNMPv3, LDAP → LDAPS."]]},{"title":"Threat actors and social engineering","dom":2,"obj":"2.1, 2.2","topics":["Actors: nation-state, organized crime, hacktivist, insider, unskilled attacker, shadow IT","Motivations: espionage, financial, disruption, ideology","Threat vectors: email, SMS, voice, removable media, supply chain, open ports","Social engineering: phishing, vishing, smishing, pretexting, BEC, watering hole, typosquatting"],"notes":["Common Types of Threat Actors","Types of Attacks","Warm Ups (Salt Typhoon)"],"lab":"Using your Salt Typhoon warm-up, write the actor type, motivation, likely vectors and two IOC types you found.","study":[["Classify Salt Typhoon: actor type, sophistication, funding and goal.","Nation-state APT: highly sophisticated, well-funded, long-term espionage against telecoms and political targets."],["What makes an insider threat hard to detect, and which control helps most?","They already have legitimate access. Least privilege plus access monitoring/UEBA helps most."],["Give an example of a supply chain attack and its mitigation.","A compromised vendor software update. Mitigate with vendor risk assessment, code signing verification and monitoring."]]},{"title":"Application and web vulnerabilities","dom":2,"obj":"2.3, 2.4","topics":["OWASP Top 10: broken access control, injection, misconfiguration, integrity failures, SSRF","SQL injection, XSS (stored/reflected), CSRF","Buffer overflow, race conditions (TOCTOU), memory injection","Threat modeling"],"notes":["Web App Security","Implementing Web App Security","Threat Modeling & Testing","Application Vulnerabilities","Unit 3 & Unit 4 Assessments"],"lab":"Take the three OWASP questions you missed in Unit 3 and write your own scenario for each category.","study":[["Broken access control vs security misconfiguration: how do you tell them apart on the exam?","Access control = users can do or see what they shouldn't (authorization logic). Misconfiguration = insecure defaults or settings, like an open bucket or default credentials."],["What is the single best defense against SQL injection?","Parameterized queries (prepared statements), backed by input validation."],["Describe proactive threat modeling in one sentence.","Mapping how attackers could abuse a system during design, then prioritizing mitigations before code is written."]]},{"title":"Malware and attack indicators","dom":2,"obj":"2.4","topics":["Malware: ransomware, trojan, worm, spyware, rootkit, logic bomb, keylogger, fileless","Password attacks: spraying, brute force, credential stuffing","Crypto attacks: downgrade, collision, birthday","Indicators: impossible travel, account lockout, resource consumption, missing logs"],"notes":["Introduction to Malware","Ransomware","Intro to Malware Analysis","Malware Analysis Techniques & Tools"],"lab":"Pull 10 failed-login events from any Splunk practice data or a log sample and decide: spraying, brute force or normal?","study":[["Password spraying vs brute force: what does each look like in the logs?","Spraying: many accounts, one or two attempts each with common passwords. Brute force: many attempts against one account, often causing lockout."],["What makes fileless malware hard to find?","It runs in memory using legitimate tools like PowerShell, leaving little on disk for signature-based AV."],["List four indicators of compromise the exam likes.","Impossible travel, account lockouts, unusual resource consumption, missing or deleted logs, out-of-cycle logging, blocked content."]]},{"title":"Mitigations + Checkpoint 2","dom":2,"obj":"2.5","topics":["Segmentation and isolation","Least privilege, access control lists","Application allow listing","Patching, encryption, monitoring","Hardening: disable ports/services, change defaults, remove unused software","Checkpoint test: Domain 2"],"notes":["Vulnerability Attacks & Risk Mgmt","Securing Servers Using Honeypots and IP Blocking"],"lab":"Write a five-step hardening checklist for a new Linux server using commands from your Linux notes.","study":[["A legacy system can't be patched. What are your options?","Compensating controls: isolate/segment it, restrict access, add monitoring, apply virtual patching via IPS/WAF."],["Why is allow listing stronger than block listing?","Only approved software runs; anything unknown is blocked by default instead of needing a signature."],["Name five hardening steps for a Windows or Linux host.","Patch, disable unused services/ports, change default passwords, enable host firewall, least-privilege accounts, enable logging, install EDR."]]},{"title":"Architecture models (lighter week)","dom":3,"obj":"3.1","topics":["Cloud: IaaS/PaaS/SaaS and shared responsibility","IaC, serverless, microservices, containers","Virtualization risks: VM escape, sprawl","ICS/SCADA, IoT, embedded, RTOS","On-prem vs cloud vs hybrid trade-offs"],"notes":["Computer Fundamentals (VMs)","Server Administration"],"lab":"Thanksgiving week: 30 minutes a day of flashcards and the weekly quiz only.","study":[["In IaaS, who patches the guest OS? In SaaS?","IaaS: the customer. SaaS: the provider."],["Why are ICS/SCADA networks usually segmented or air-gapped?","Availability is critical and they often run legacy systems that can't be patched quickly."],["What security benefit does Infrastructure as Code give?","Consistent, repeatable, reviewable deployments that reduce misconfiguration and drift."]],"light":true},{"title":"Securing enterprise infrastructure","dom":3,"obj":"3.2","topics":["Firewalls (L4/L7, NGFW), WAF, UTM","IDS vs IPS, inline vs tap","Fail-open vs fail-closed","802.1X, NAC, port security","VPN, IPsec, TLS, SD-WAN, SASE, jump servers, proxies"],"notes":["Introduction to Computer Network","Server Administration"],"lab":"Write three firewall rules in order (allow HTTPS to web server, allow SSH from admin subnet, deny all) and explain why order matters.","study":[["PBQ drill: put these rules in the right order — deny all; allow 443 to 10.0.0.5; allow 22 from 10.0.9.0/24.","Specific allows first (443 to web server, 22 from admin subnet), implicit/explicit deny all last. Firewalls match top-down."],["IDS vs IPS: placement and what each can do.","IDS is passive (tap/span), alerts only. IPS sits inline and can block."],["What does 802.1X need to work?","Supplicant (client), authenticator (switch/AP) and authentication server (usually RADIUS)."]]},{"title":"Data protection and resilience + Checkpoint 3","dom":3,"obj":"3.3, 3.4","topics":["Data types and classifications","Data states: at rest, in transit, in use","Protection: encryption, hashing, masking, tokenization, DLP","Resilience: HA, clustering, load balancing, RAID","Backups, sites (hot/warm/cold), RPO/RTO","Checkpoint test: Domain 3"],"notes":["Unit 4 Assessment (load balancer / DDoS)"],"lab":"For the lounge's POS and guest list, write how each would be protected at rest, in transit and in use.","study":[["RPO vs RTO in plain words.","RPO: how much data you can afford to lose (time since last good backup). RTO: how long until the system is back up."],["Tokenization vs masking.","Tokenization swaps data for a random token mapped in a secure vault (reversible by the vault). Masking hides part of the data for display."],["Which backup combo restores fastest from the fewest sets: full+incremental or full+differential?","Full + differential: you need only the last full and the last differential."]]},{"title":"Hardening, wireless and vulnerability management","dom":4,"obj":"4.1, 4.2, 4.3","topics":["Secure baselines, mobile (MDM, BYOD, COPE, CYOD)","Wireless: WPA3, SAE, RADIUS, EAP","Asset management and disposal (sanitize, destroy, certify)","Vulnerability scanning: credentialed, false positives, CVSS, CVE","Pen testing and recon: passive vs active"],"notes":["Ethical Hacking & Reconnaissance","Active Reconnaissance","Scanning & Enumeration","Unit 2 Assessment (CVSS)"],"lab":"Run an nmap SYN scan (-sS) and a version scan (-sV) on a lab VM. Note what each reveals.","study":[["Which CVSS score do you adjust for your own environment?","The environmental score."],["Passive vs active reconnaissance, with an example of each.","Passive: OSINT, WHOIS, LinkedIn, no contact with target. Active: port scanning, banner grabbing, touching the target."],["Why is a credentialed scan preferred?","It logs in and sees installed software and configs, giving more accurate results with fewer false positives."]]},{"title":"Monitoring, SIEM and identity (lighter week)","dom":4,"obj":"4.4, 4.5, 4.6","topics":["Logs, SIEM correlation, alerting, SCAP, NetFlow","Email security: SPF, DKIM, DMARC","EDR/XDR, DLP, UEBA","IAM: provisioning, SSO, SAML, OAuth, OpenID Connect, LDAP","MFA factors, PAM, just-in-time access"],"notes":["Intro to Logs","Log Management and SIEM Optimization","Logs in SIEM","Splunk Fundamentals","SIEM Workflows","Identity and Access Management"],"lab":"Holiday week: build flashcards for SPF/DKIM/DMARC and SAML vs OAuth vs OIDC, then take the weekly quiz.","study":[["SPF, DKIM, DMARC: one line each.","SPF: which servers may send for a domain. DKIM: cryptographic signature on the message. DMARC: policy telling receivers what to do when SPF/DKIM fail, plus reporting."],["SAML vs OAuth.","SAML: authentication/SSO assertions for web apps. OAuth: authorization, letting an app access resources on your behalf without your password."],["What does a SIEM add beyond collecting logs?","Normalization, correlation across sources, alerting and dashboards for investigation."]],"light":true},{"title":"Incident response and forensics + Checkpoint 4","dom":4,"obj":"4.7, 4.8, 4.9","topics":["IR process: preparation, detection, analysis, containment, eradication, recovery, lessons learned","Tabletop exercises and simulations","Forensics: order of volatility, chain of custody, legal hold, acquisition","Automation and SOAR playbooks","Investigation data sources"],"notes":["Incident Response & Malware Protection","SIEM Workflows"],"lab":"Write a one-page ransomware playbook using the seven IR phases.","study":[["List the IR phases in order.","Preparation, detection, analysis, containment, eradication, recovery, lessons learned."],["Why capture RAM before imaging the disk?","Order of volatility: memory is lost at power-off, disk persists."],["What does chain of custody protect?","The integrity and admissibility of evidence by documenting every person who handled it and when."]]},{"title":"Governance, risk and compliance + Checkpoint 5","dom":5,"obj":"5.1 – 5.6","topics":["Policies, standards, procedures, guidelines","Risk: register, appetite, tolerance, SLE/ARO/ALE","Risk treatment: accept, avoid, transfer, mitigate","Third-party risk: SLA, MOU, MSA, SOW, NDA, right to audit","Compliance, privacy roles (controller, processor), audits","Security awareness and phishing simulations"],"notes":["Risk Assessment","Vulnerability Attacks & Risk Mgmt","Unit 2 Assessment"],"lab":"Build a five-row risk register for 11:Eleven (e.g. POS breach, guest-list leak) with SLE, ARO, ALE and treatment.","study":[["Asset value $80,000, exposure factor 50%, ARO 2. Calculate ALE.","SLE = 80,000 × 0.5 = 40,000. ALE = 40,000 × 2 = $80,000."],["Give an example of each risk treatment.","Accept: document and live with it. Avoid: stop the risky activity. Transfer: cyber insurance or outsourcing. Mitigate: add controls."],["Policy vs standard vs procedure.","Policy: high-level intent (must). Standard: specific mandatory requirement. Procedure: step-by-step instructions."]]},{"title":"Full practice exams and final review","dom":0,"obj":"All domains","topics":["Two full 90-question timed practice exams","Review every miss, re-quiz until the review queue is empty","Re-drill weakest domain from the Progress tab","Exam logistics: ID, Pearson VUE check-in, 90 minutes, PBQs first or flag and return"],"notes":["All Block Reviews","Unit 2, 3 and 4 Assessments"],"lab":"Target 85%+ on both practice exams before sitting the real one.","study":[["What's your strategy for PBQs on exam day?","Skim them, flag and skip if long, answer all multiple choice, then return with remaining time."],["Which domain carries the most weight?","Security operations (28%)."],["What score passes Security+?","750 on a 100–900 scale."]]}],"qCount":101});
